00:00:54.039 --> 00:01:04.260
So tremolo on the guitar&nbsp;
is like a magic trick it creates the illusion that&nbsp;there are two instruments playing at the same&nbsp;
time and the way it works is that basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:13.350
the thumb plays its own part like this. A simple&nbsp;arpeggiation and then that's gonna keep on going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:21.450
but then we're gonna add the fingers rapidly&nbsp;
plugging in between each thumb stroke like this

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:36.990
and it creates the most beautiful haunting&nbsp;
murmuring melody in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.390
so to break that down further, between every thumb&nbsp;
stroke we have to fit in these three fingers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:07.150
this order ring middle index so tremolo is when&nbsp;
we play thumb ring middle index thumb ring middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:15.640
index on repeat.  Now in classical guitar lingo&nbsp;we give the fingers special names this is P I M&nbsp;and A

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.460
So Pema Pima whatever you want to call it&nbsp;
but for the rest of the video I'll be referring&nbsp;to the fingers by those terms.&nbsp;

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:28.300
Okay so to learn&nbsp;this technique let's start by putting all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:35.260
our fingers in our right hand except the pinky on&nbsp;the first string, that's the high E string and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:41.800
sure when you do this that your thumb actually&nbsp;
goes to the left of the fingers so it's not inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:48.580
the hand hidden it's to the left of the fingers&nbsp;
from your point of view. Also the wrist shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:55.630
